                    %mo →
                    %mi ℂ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \epsilon_h:Sp(A)\to\mathbb{C}
              defined above.
          %p
            The adjunction induces an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/idempotent+monad"} idempotent monad
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Meas
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Meas
            (the
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/zero-one+measure#the_monad_of_zeroone_measures"} monad of zero-one measures
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi σ
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mo *
                  %mi Alg
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma C^*Alg
            as well (or equivalently, an idempotent
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/comonad"} comonad
            on
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                    %mi C
                    %mo *
                    %msup
                      %mi Alg
                      %mi op
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma C*Alg^{op}
          %p
            As usual with idempotent adjunctions, we have that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fixed+point+of+an+adjunction"} center
            is equivalently given by
          %ul
            %li
              The full subcategory of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Meas
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Meas
              whose objects are those spaces for which the unit
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi η
                    %mo :
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi Sp
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi ℒ
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta:X\to Sp(\mathcal{L}^\infty(X))
              is an isomorphism. These are sometimes called
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sober+measurable+spaces"} sober measurable spaces
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sober+topological+spaces"} sober topological spaces
            %li
              The full subcategory of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mo *
                    %mi Alg
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma C^*Alg
              whose objects are those σ-C*-algebras for which the counit
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϵ
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mi ℒ
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Sp
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \epsilon:A\to \mathcal{L}^\infty(Sp(A))
              is an isomorphism. These are sometimes called
              %strong spatial
              or
              %strong concrete
              σ-C*-algebras.
          %p
            Because of this, sometimes one calls the monad on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Meas
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Meas
            the
            = succeed "." do
              %em sobrification monad
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Loomis-Sikorski+duality"} Loomis-Sikorski duality
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#equivalence_with_loomissikorski_duality"} below
          %h2#further_results Further results
          %h3#equivalence_with_loomissikorski_duality Equivalence with Loomis-Sikorski duality
          %p
            In ordinary
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measure+theory"} measure theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measurable+subsets"} measurable subsets
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\subseteq X
            are “included” in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measurable+functions"} measurable functions
            via the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/indicator+functions"} indicator functions
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mn 1
                      %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1_A
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            (for example, by taking suprema of linear combinations of indicators).
          %p
            We can abstract this idea of “functions with values
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
            and
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projection"} projection
            elements of a C*-algebra, that is, those elements
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi h
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h\in A
            such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi h
                  %mo =
                  %mi h
                  %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h=h*
            (self-adjoint, generalizing real-valued) and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi h
                  %mi h
                  %mo =
                  %mi h
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h h=h
            (idempotent). As one can show, the projection elements of a commutative σ-C*algebra indeed form a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%CF%83-complete+Boolean+algebra"} σ-complete Boolean algebra
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Loomis-Sikorski+duality"} Loomis-Sikorski duality
          %p
            We therefore have an equivalence of categories between σ-C*algebras and σ-complete Boolean algebras, commuting with the left and right-adjoints as in the following diagram.
